      "Uniformly slowed narration sounds condescending, and children detect condescension with unnerving accuracy. What actually helps is slowing the *hard* parts: a new word, a long sentence, the moment the plot turns. The easy connective tissue between them can run at ordinary speed. A narrator who slows for everything teaches a child that the whole text is difficult, which is both untrue and discouraging.",
      "The pauses matter more than the words-per-minute figure. A young listener builds the scene during silence, not during speech — the half-second after \"and behind the door was\" is when the picture actually forms. Cut that half-second to keep the file short and the story arrives faster and lands less. When we tuned AXTO's read-aloud, lengthening sentence-boundary pauses did more for whether children finished a story than any change to the voice itself.",
      "There is a related mistake worth avoiding: matching pace to the child's reading level. Listening comprehension runs years ahead of decoding, which is the entire reason read-aloud works. A seven-year-old who reads at a seven-year-old level can follow a story written well above it, as long as the delivery is good. Slowing narration to their decoding speed wastes the gap that made the exercise valuable.",
      "## What the highlight has to get right to teach anything",
      "Synchronised highlighting — the word lighting up as it is spoken — is the feature that quietly turns listening into pre-reading. It is also the feature most often shipped slightly wrong, in ways that make it useless or worse.",
      "Drift is the first problem. A highlight that runs a few hundred milliseconds ahead or behind the voice does not merely fail to teach; it actively teaches the wrong pairing, attaching a sound to the word beside the one that made it. Children do not correct for the lag the way adults do. They believe what they are shown.",
      "Granularity is the second. Sentence-level highlighting looks tidier and teaches almost nothing, because the whole point is binding one sound to one printed shape. Word-level is the minimum. It is harder, it exposes every timing flaw, and it is the difference between a decorative feature and a phonics one.",
      "Scroll behaviour is the third and the most often neglected. If the page jumps when the highlight reaches the bottom, the child loses their place at the exact moment they were following most closely. Keeping the active line at a steady height, and moving the text underneath it, costs nothing and preserves the thread.",
      "Get those three right and something quietly useful happens: a child who cannot yet read is repeatedly shown which mark on the page makes which sound, inside a story they chose, with no lesson attached. That is incidental phonics, and it is the most painless kind there is.",
      "## The voice a family already has",
      "A read-aloud feature that speaks only English serves only some families, and the ones it excludes are frequently the ones with the least access to other read-aloud sources.",
      "The architecture matters here more than the voice quality. Browser-native speech is already installed on the device, already speaks whatever language the family set the phone to, and costs nothing per play. It is not as warm as a premium neural voice. It is available to everyone, immediately, in Urdu and Tagalog and Portuguese, which a premium voice catalogue usually is not.",
      "So AXTO narrates through the reader's own language by default and offers better voices as an upgrade rather than a gate. The consequence is that a family reading in a language with no commercial audiobook market gets narration on the same day as everyone else. That is worth more than the last increment of naturalness in English.",
      "There is a caveat worth stating plainly: browser voices in some languages are genuinely poor, and a bad voice in a child's home language is not a kindness. Where the device voice is unusable, saying so and offering the text plainly is more honest than shipping something that will teach a child their language sounds like a machine.",
      "## Where narration should stop",
      "None of this is an argument that a device should do the reading. It is an argument that when the device is doing the reading anyway — in the car, in the waiting room, on the evening a parent is working late — it should do it well.",
      "The evidence on shared reading keeps pointing at the same active ingredient, and it is not the words. It is the interruption: the child asking what a word means, the adult asking what happens next, the two of them looking at the same picture. No narration reproduces that, and a product claiming otherwise is selling something.",
      "The honest framing is narrower and still worth having. Narration keeps the story stream running on the days it would otherwise stop. It gives a child a way to meet books above their decoding level. It carries the fifteenth reading of a favourite when the adult has nothing left. And on the evenings a parent is available, the right thing for the software to do is get out of the way.",
