      "## The gap that makes read-aloud work",
      "The whole case for reading aloud to a child who can already read rests on one fact, and it is worth stating precisely: listening comprehension runs well ahead of reading comprehension for most of primary school.",
      "A seven-year-old decoding simple sentences can follow, enjoy and remember a story written for ten-year-olds, provided somebody else does the decoding. The understanding is already there. What is not yet there is the mechanical fluency to get the words off the page fast enough for meaning to survive the trip.",
      "Those two lines converge somewhere in the upper primary years for most children, and later for some. Until they meet, every hour of read-aloud is delivering vocabulary, sentence structures and ideas the child could not yet reach alone — which is to say, precisely the material they will need when their decoding catches up.",
      "Stopping at the moment a child reads independently means restricting them to the simplest texts available to them at exactly the age when their appetite for complexity is growing fastest. It feels like graduation and functions as a downgrade.",
      "## What listening does that decoding cannot",
      "The difference is about where attention goes.",
      "When a child listens, essentially all of their capacity is free for meaning: the plot, the joke, the character who is obviously lying, the new word whose sense arrives from the situation around it. Nothing is being spent on mechanics.",
