      "## Work out which reluctance you are dealing with",
      "Reluctance is a symptom with several different causes, and the interventions that help one make another worse. It is worth a few weeks of watching before deciding what to do.",
      "The first kind is mechanical. Reading is genuinely hard work — the decoding is not yet automatic, and every page costs real effort. This child avoids reading for the same reason anyone avoids something exhausting. Encouragement does nothing; easier text, more of it, and audio support do.",
      "The second is associative. The child can read perfectly well and has learned that reading means being corrected, tested, or compared. They are not avoiding the book; they are avoiding the interaction that comes with it. The fix is to remove every trace of assessment from the situation for a long time — months, not days.",
      "The third is competitive. Reading is fine and other things are better. A screen offers faster reward, friends are outside, the game has a raid at seven. This child is not damaged and does not need repairing; they need a book good enough to compete, and the bar for that is genuinely high.",
      "The fourth is content. They have never been handed anything they wanted. Given only fiction about feelings when what they want is a detailed illustrated book about submarines, they have reasonably concluded reading is not for them.",
