      "## The distinction that makes accommodations work",
      "Most of the advice above is sound and it is missing the distinction that decides whether any of it helps: the difference between access and instruction.",
      "Access means the child can get at the content. Text-to-speech reading a history chapter aloud gives a dyslexic child the history. Without it, they are excluded from a subject they are perfectly capable of, for reasons that have nothing to do with history.",
      "Instruction means the child is learning to read. That is a separate activity with separate time, and no amount of text-to-speech advances it.",
      "The failure mode is using one to replace the other. A child given audio for everything has full access and receives no reading instruction, and the gap widens under a system that looks supportive. A child denied audio until their reading improves is locked out of the whole curriculum in the meantime, and falls behind in every subject while waiting.",
      "The answer is both, kept separate and both protected. Audio and read-aloud for content, so the child keeps learning everything else at their actual level. Structured, systematic, explicit reading instruction in its own slot, at the right level, every day. Neither is optional and neither substitutes.",
      "## What structured instruction means in practice",
      "For children who struggle with decoding, the evidence points consistently in one direction, and it is worth being concrete about what it involves rather than gesturing at multisensory approaches.",
      "It is explicit: the letter-sound relationships are taught directly, not discovered from context. It is systematic: the sequence runs from simple to complex in a planned order rather than following whatever the current book contains. It is cumulative: each step builds on what has been secured. It includes plenty of practice reading text that contains what has been taught, so the child experiences the rules working.",
      "It is also, for children with dyslexia, a matter of considerably more repetition than a typical programme provides. Not different in kind — the same instruction, more of it, more often, for longer. The most common failure is not the wrong method but too little of the right one.",
      "Guessing strategies — predicting from pictures, from the first letter, from context — should be actively discouraged for these children. They produce apparent progress in the early years, because early books are predictable, and they collapse around age eight when the text stops being guessable. A great deal of what presents as a sudden decline at that age is a guessing strategy running out of road.",
