      "Phonological awareness is not one skill but a sequence, and teaching them out of order is the most common reason a child appears not to be progressing.",
      "The broadest level comes first: hearing that a sentence is made of separate words, and that a word can be broken into beats. Clapping syllables in names is this, and most four-year-olds manage it.",
      "Rhyme and alliteration come next — noticing that cat and hat end the same way, that six words in a row start with the same sound. This is the level nursery rhymes have been training for centuries.",
      "Then onset and rime: splitting cat into /c/ and /at/. This is a genuinely useful halfway house, because it lets a child work with word families before they can handle individual sounds.",
      "And last, and hardest, phonemic awareness: hearing that cat is /c/ /a/ /t/, three separate sounds, and being able to blend them back together, take one away, or swap one for another.",
      "That final level is the one most closely tied to learning to read, and it is also the one that does not develop on its own for a large minority of children. Most need it taught. Some need it taught for a long time.",
